<section class="section section-tight">
  <div class="container two-col-grid">
    <div class="content-panel">
      <h2 class="section-title section-title-glow">Tech vs. traditional enterprise — the GC comp gap</h2>
      <p>A General Counsel at a growth-stage tech company earns $380K–$680K total comp, including base, bonus, and RSU equity grants. The same title at a traditional enterprise or mid-size non-tech company earns $240K–$420K. The gap — $140K–$260K — is almost entirely equity. Tech companies grant RSUs to GC-level legal leaders as a core compensation component. Traditional employers do not.</p>
      <p>In-house counsel moving from traditional companies into tech GC or CLO roles face a positioning challenge: tech companies calibrate legal leadership on deal velocity, regulatory risk reduction, privacy/data governance, and how quickly legal enables rather than slows product and business decisions. The experience often transfers; the communication of that experience in tech-friendly terms often does not without deliberate preparation.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-panel">
      <h2 class="section-title section-title-glow">In-house counsel offer negotiation</h2>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>Quantify business impact</strong> — Deal velocity improved, regulatory risk mitigation value, compliance program ROI, litigation cost reduction — these numbers move legal compensation conversations</li>
        <li><strong>Equity at AGC and above</strong> — RSU grants are standard at tech companies at Associate GC level and above; negotiate the grant, cliff, and refresh explicitly</li>
        <li><strong>Specialization premium</strong> — Privacy/data governance, IP, M&amp;A, securities, and regulatory specializations command premiums; name yours in the negotiation</li>
        <li><strong>Use ACC and MLA benchmarks</strong> — The ACC Chief Legal Officer Survey and Major, Lindsey &amp; Africa reports carry credibility in legal compensation conversations</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

