      <h2 class="section-title section-title-glow">In-house counsel salary — common questions</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-grid">
      <details class="faq-card" open>
        <summary>What is a good in-house counsel salary in 2026?</summary>
        <p>A good total compensation for Senior Counsel at a tech company is $215K–$330K. At a financial services firm, $200K–$320K. At a traditional enterprise, $145K–$225K. For an Associate General Counsel at a tech company, $265K–$420K. The GC comp gap between tech and traditional companies is particularly large — a GC at a growth-stage tech company earns $380K–$680K, vs. $240K–$420K at a comparable traditional enterprise, driven almost entirely by equity grants.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-card">
        <summary>What does a General Counsel earn in 2026?</summary>
        <p>A General Counsel at a mid-size tech or high-growth company earns $380K–$680K total comp in 2026, including base, annual bonus, and RSU equity. At financial services firms, $360K–$650K. At traditional enterprises, $240K–$420K. At large public technology companies, CLO/GC total comp reaches $500K–$1.2M+, with equity grants comprising a substantial portion of that figure.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-card">
        <summary>How do in-house lawyers negotiate higher compensation?</summary>
        <p>Three specific levers: (1) Business impact quantification — deal velocity improvements, regulatory risk reduction value, compliance program ROI, litigation cost avoidance are the numbers that move legal compensation conversations beyond title-based benchmarks. (2) Equity — RSU grants are negotiable at AGC, DGC, and GC levels; candidates who only negotiate base and bonus leave significant value on the table. (3) Specialization premium — privacy, data governance, IP, M&amp;A, and securities expertise commands a market rate; use it explicitly as leverage. See the full <a href="/how-to-negotiate-salary/">salary negotiation guide</a> for scripts.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-card">
        <summary>When should in-house counsel hire a career coach?</summary>
        <p>Four high-ROI situations: (1) Moving from a traditional company into a tech company GC or CLO role — the positioning language around enabling business velocity and regulatory strategy is different from traditional legal department leadership. (2) Targeting a GC role for the first time — the interview narrative shifts from legal function management to C-suite business partnership. (3) Offer in hand from a tech company where equity is a major component. (4) Title has not kept pace with expanded scope and organizational influence. Career coaching at Askia averages a $47K salary increase in compensation-focused engagements.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

